<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project>

  <!--
    Repo-wide build policy, imported automatically into every project under the repository root.

    Before this file existed, these properties were copy-pasted into the individual project files.
    Nothing compared the copies, so they drifted silently: the same divergence is what let six
    package versions fork across the test suites (see Directory.Packages.props).

    Drift is not only two projects disagreeing about a value — it is also half of them not stating it
    at all, which is how ImplicitUsings arrived here. It was enabled in ten of the twenty projects and
    absent from the other ten, so whether a file needed `using System;` depended on which project it
    was compiled into, and a new project inherited whichever neighbour it was copied from.

    A project that genuinely needs a different value overrides it locally — project files are
    evaluated after this one, so a local setting still wins. Overrides are therefore visible as
    deliberate exceptions rather than invisible as copies. No project overrides Nullable today:
    every one of them compiles with nullable reference types enabled.

    That overridability is precisely why the warning gate is NOT here. TreatWarningsAsErrors lives in
    Directory.Build.targets, which is imported AFTER a project's own body and therefore cannot be
    overridden by one: a project quietly setting it false would not be a visible exception, it would be
    a hole in the gate every other project is held to. The split is the rule for adding a property here
    — this file for a value a project may legitimately differ on, the targets file for one it may not.

    LangVersion is deliberately ABSENT. It tracks the target framework, so net10.0 already selects
    C# 14; the five test suites that used to name it spelled it `latest`, which resolves to whatever
    the compiler on the machine happens to support and so differs between a developer box and a CI
    runner with a newer SDK.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Nothing here is a NuGet package unless it says so. The SDK's default is the opposite, which made every
    application, example, utility and shared library packable by accident: a `dotnet pack` at the root
    produced a dozen packages nobody publishes, each with no id, description or licence beyond what the
    SDK could infer from the project name.

    ihcclient overrides this, and is the only project that should: it is the one with packaging actually
    configured, staging its findings-export schema into the package. The seven suites used to state this
    for themselves and now inherit it from here.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Assembly identity shared by everything here. Version is NOT among these: it is the one piece of
    identity that genuinely differs per project, so a project that has one states it for itself.

    It states ONLY Version. AssemblyVersion and FileVersion used to be spelled out beside it as
    $(Version), which reads like a load-bearing line and is not one — the SDK's GetAssemblyVersion target
    already derives both from $(Version) (measured: ihcclient 0.8.1 -> 0.8.1.0). They are gone, and adding
    them back to a new project would restate the default rather than pin anything.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Authors>mmc41</Authors>
    <Copyright>Apache License 2.0</Copyright>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Roslyn code analysis. The built-in .NET analyzers are on by default for net10.0; these two properties
    are the part the SDK does not decide for us.

    AnalysisLevel is pinned rather than left at its 'latest' default. Left floating, every .NET SDK upgrade
    can enable rules nobody opted into, and because warnings are errors here that lands as a red build
    rather than as advice. This is the same reason package versions are pinned centrally: a version — or a
    rule set — that is stated once cannot drift. Raise it deliberately, with the new rules' findings fixed
    in the same change.

    AnalysisModeSecurity turns the whole security category on. Measured before enabling: one finding in the
    entire repository, so the category costs nothing to hold at maximum and would otherwise report a real
    XML-parsing weakness only as an IDE suggestion. The other categories stay at Default and the rules
    worth having from them are opted into individually in .globalconfig, because the two largest rules a
    category mode would bring in (CA1707 on test names, CA2007 on ConfigureAwait) do not apply repo-wide — and
    CA2007 demands the opposite of what the GUI's architecture rules forbid (ADR-001). CA2007 is enabled for
    the SDK alone, in ihcclient/analyser.config.

    AnalysisMode is raised here for EVERY project in the repository — the SDK, the two Avalonia applications,
    the shared bootstrap libraries, the examples, the utilities and the test suites alike. Recommended is the
    middle of the ladder (None < Default < Minimum < Recommended < All) and a strict superset of Minimum; it is
    the level at which a latent culture, allocation, disposal or contract defect is reported at build time
    rather than found in a run. The rules a project declines out of that mode are turned off one at a time,
    each with its reason, in .globalconfig for the decisions that hold repo-wide and in ihcclient/analyser.config
    for the ones that are the SDK's alone — which also names the one rule scoped by path outside it.

    Because tests/Directory.Build.props imports this file rather than replacing it, the suites inherit that
    value; the declines that are theirs alone are path-scoped in tests/.editorconfig. Holding the suites at a
    lower mode was tried and reverted: what it saved was performance and API-shape rules aimed at code that
    ships, but it also switched off the culture rules, which nothing opts into by name and which were finding
    real locale-dependent defects in test code.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AnalysisLevel>10.0</AnalysisLevel>
    <AnalysisModeSecurity>All</AnalysisModeSecurity>
    <AnalysisMode>Recommended</AnalysisMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
